<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pattadakal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pattadakal is a village on the Malaprabha river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagalkot-district">Bagalkot district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with a group of 7th- and 8th-century temples built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>. Used for royal coronations, it combines northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Nagara</a> and southern Dravida styles; the largest, the Virupaksha temple, was built by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokamahadevi">Lokamahadevi</a> around 740. The group is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pattadakal.jpg" alt="Pattadakal" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nithin bolar k · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pattadakal_000.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
